= La cuenta de Moscú

The envelope arrives every January, the way it has for decades: Moscow pays its friends, quietly, in a currency that funds more presses than dues ever will. But this year the courier carries a message with the money, delivered in the roundabout grammar of people who never say the thing directly — the comrades in Moscow have *noticed* the Mexican party's line on Czechoslovakia, on the dissidents, on the awkward habit of criticizing the socialist camp in print. The subsidy, the message implies without implying, is not unconditional. Friends who embarrass friends are still friends. But.

The Party has built its whole distinct identity on the heresy: the communist party that condemned the tanks in Prague, that kept a conscience along with its party card. That identity has never before had a price tag attached. Now it does, and the treasury — which the presses and the organizers empty every month — feels the weight of the number.

- @mantener_herejia: Keep the heresy and lose the money. Independence was never going to be free, and a party that can be bought is not independent.
- @moderar_criticas: Soften the criticism, keep the subsidy. The presses do not run on principle, and a bankrupt party helps no one.
- @buscar_otras_fuentes: Refuse the choice — build the self-financing the independence line always implied you would need: dues, the fusion's resources, the movement's own caja.

@mantener_herejia
on-arrival: ax_sovietico -= 4; moscow_relation -= 15; funds -= 4; prestigio_socialismo += 4; fac_ortodoxos -= 3; unity += 2

The January envelope stops coming, and the treasury feels it immediately — a press that runs less, an organizer who goes unpaid, a caja that was never fat and is now honest. But the identity holds, and the identity turns out to be worth something the money never was: the Party is the one on the Mexican left that no one can accuse of taking Moscow's orders, and in the years when the Soviet star is fading that independence ages into an asset. The ortodoxos who loved the subsidy grieve; the renovadores who hated the leash exhale. You have bought your own name back at retail.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@moderar_criticas
on-arrival: ax_sovietico += 3; moscow_relation += 6; funds += 5; prestigio_socialismo -= 4; fac_nueva_izquierda -= 3; des_nueva += 4; unity -= 2

The criticism goes quieter — not recanted, just *managed*, the sharp editorials spiked, the awkward solidarities muted — and the January envelope keeps its weight. The presses run; the organizers eat; the caja survives another year. And something intangible thins: the heresy that made the Party interesting becomes, by degrees, the caution that makes it ordinary, and the young who joined for the conscience notice the volume dropping. Moscow's money was always cheap. Its price was only ever charged later, in the currency the Party could least spare.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@buscar_otras_fuentes
choose-if: members >= 15 or fusion_prog >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: Self-financing needs a base big enough to fund itself — dues from members you have not yet organized.
on-arrival: moscow_relation -= 8; prestigio_socialismo += 3; dues += 1; unity += 2; fac_renovadores += 2

You take the hard road the independence line always implied and rarely walked: a real dues system, the fusion's pooled resources, the movement's own caja fed by the sectors it organizes. It is slower than an envelope and it does not fully replace it — the first lean years are lean — but it changes the Party's relationship to its own principles from rented to owned. A party financed by its members answers to its members, which is either the oldest cliché in the movement or the whole point of it, depending on whether anyone actually does it. You have decided to actually do it.

- @post_event: Continuar.
